y was second in " [:a {:href "/results/38095"} "1928"] ". But Hungary had been European Champion in 1926, 1927, and 1931, and was favored in LA."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The gold medal came down to the match between the two favorites, with Hungary winning the match 6-2, and going undefeated in the round-robin to win gold. Germany and the United States tied with two wins, one tie, and one loss, but Germany was awarded the silver on goal differential. From 1932-1964, Hungary would win water polo gold in five of seven Olympic Games."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Brazil lost its first two matches, the second 7-3 against Germany. After the match, the players attacked the Hungarian referee " [:a {:href "/athletes/2304981"} "Béla Komjádi"] ", which had to be broken up by the police. Brazil was suspended and forfeited their final two games."] "\n\n" [:p {} "United States’ player " [:a {:href "/athletes/54911"} "Phil Daubenspeck"] " scored 14 goals during the tournament, which stood as an Olympic water polo record for one Games until " [:a {:href "/results/38432"} "1968"] ", when the Netherlands’ " [:a {:href "/athletes/54566"} "Nico van der Voet"] " scored 33 goals in Mexico City."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button614990", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button614990').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {}] [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Team"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Goals"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {:class "accordion-toggle", :data-toggle "collapse", :data-target ".team-members-559646"} [:div {:class "glyphicon glyphicon-plus"}]] [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Hungary"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "31"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold